Z||000000||b lo 0||08-11-2001||03:31 PM||||The GT refuses to start when the engine is hot, forcing me to open the decklid and wait a few minutes when pumping gas or stuff like that.  The motor has been operating at normal temps, and does not overheat, even in 90 degree GA weather.  Hey, she's driveable now, but this is a little annoying.  Thanks, guys!<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">       <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com.com" TARGET=_blank>Audio Imagery</A>, budget-priced car audio supplies from people like Monster Cable, Sony & more!||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-11-2001||03:49 PM||||I am assuming that you mean that shes cranking  over ok when she is hot.If so check to see if you have a spark while cranking.If answer is yes ,It maybe fuel related. Let us know so that we could figure out what steps we have to take to figure this out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||08-11-2001||04:46 PM||||You didnt say if it was cranking and not starting or not cranking.<BR>Hot start problems can be fun to find.<BR>If its not cranking I would go for the starter they get weak after years of use and heat and dont always spin up enough when hot<P>If its cranking fine I would replace the module. They hate the heat.||||reg||1
Z||000003||b lo 0||08-11-2001||05:51 PM||||It turns over great, but does not start.  I have a feeling this is closely related to the weak spark problem I noted before along with the Code 12 light.  The ignition module is new, as are the spark plugs, wires, cap, rotor, bobbin coil, and ignition coil.  I'm not exactly in a rush to fix this, I'm just glad I can drive it now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Thanks for the ideas, guys.  I will wrap the fuel line with line insulation and see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Lisa 86 2m4||08-11-2001||10:49 PM||||It could even be something as simeple as the starter is getting too hot, cure is a heat shield. Or, what helped with my car was to replace the positive battery cable and battery. I recently moved to Florida, and even though my battery was fairly new, I just think the constant heat wears on everything, and my car started having the same difficulty. I hated stopping off anywhere for a short while for fear my car wouldn't start again until it cooled down.||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||08-11-2001||11:39 PM||||There we go again. Lets not read the replies.<BR>He did say it cranks just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-12-2001||12:10 AM||||read wire service in the ecm & electric section of my cave.<P>V6 coil power runs thru C500.... <P>The whole car runs thru the single large terminal below it.<P>Since you've replaced the majority of the ignition, a wire problem is a real posibility. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||08-13-2001||02:37 AM||||You didn't mention anything w/ regard to the fuel side of things.  On the Fiero, the engineering team used a oil pressure switch as a back-up to the fuel pump relay.  When the car is hot, the oil is hot and takes more cranking to reach the needed pressure to turn the fuel pump on (or in your case cooler oil).  To test it just turn the ignition to the 'on' position with the car off and listen for the fuel pump.  Do this with the engine hot as the relay may only fail when hot.  The relay sits in the eng bay, behind the driver so it gets real warm over there (exhaust & cat).  My 1st GT had the same exact symptom and this was the cause. The approximate cooling time here in south Florida was a 10 minute wait before restart.  GL<P>Edited for last comment - wait time<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Galen||08-13-2001||04:25 AM||||Could the car being hot(motor, start, trans, etc) cause it not to turn over?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"With one drop of blood he realized his own mortallity, it was then he saw the true face of God. For he is not someone watching down on us, but he is in us." - Galen 2001</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||b lo 0||08-13-2001||10:53 AM||||Hey guys, thanks for the responses.  I double-checked the C500, it's hunky-dory, I'll put some contact cleaner on it.  The fuel pump runs, but I'm with Ogre, I think it's the ignition, because it acts like it's not getting spark.  I'll replace the positive cable, the terminal is new, see if that helps.  The oil pressure switch is new, and the fuel pump relay was checked.  I'll print out Ogre's cave stuff.  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000010||b lo 0||08-13-2001||05:11 PM||||Cleaned up C500 and checked fusible links, they're all OK.  Here's the funky thing-when the distributor is out of the engine with ground clip attached, the spark is fantastic.  When I put the distributor back into the car, ground clip or no, the spark all but dies.  Electromagnetic interference?  Heat soak?  PLEASE help!  I got eaten alive by a Volkswagen Golf last night!  That's how bad it is.  Maybe this will help paint a better picture of my problem.  Thanks for the help, guys!||||reg||1
